{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "# Excelsior Scholarship: Replication and QuantCrit Extension\n\n**Part A** replicates Howard (2026) for public institutions: counts (not logs), institution and year fixed effects, standard errors clustered by institution, 2016 as the event-study reference year, treatment in 2017 (and 2018 as a robustness check), and covariates of logged tuition and lagged county unemployment.\n\n**Part B** is the QuantCrit-informed extension: every IPEDS category, three outcome measures (counts, composition, population-adjusted), group-specific pre-trend tests, institutional context (CUNY vs. SUNY), and a sensitivity check showing how sample decisions change the estimate.\n\nBefore running: at the end of your data-cleaning notebook, run `panel.to_csv(\"panel_clean.csv\", index=False)`."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport statsmodels.formula.api as smf\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\npd.set_option(\"display.float_format\", \"{:.3f}\".format)\n\npanel = pd.read_csv(\"panel_clean.csv\", dtype={\"fips\": str})\n\n# short names (ignored if you already renamed them)\npanel = panel.rename(columns={\n    \"Grand total\": \"ftft\",\n    \"Number of full-time first-time undergraduates awarded Pell grants\": \"pell\",\n    \"Published in-state tuition and fees\": \"tuition\",\n    \"Black or African American total\": \"black\",\n    \"Hispanic total\": \"hispanic\",\n    \"White total\": \"white\",\n    \"Asian total\": \"asian\",\n    \"American Indian or Alaska Native total\": \"aian\",\n    \"Native Hawaiian or Other Pacific Islander total\": \"nhpi\",\n    \"Two or more races total\": \"two_plus\",\n    \"Race/ethnicity unknown total\": \"unknown\",\n    \"Nonresident alien total\": \"nonresident\",\n    \"U.S. Nonresident total\": \"nonresident\",\n})\n\npanel[\"ny\"] = (panel[\"state\"] == \"NY\").astype(int)\npanel[\"log_tuition\"] = np.log(panel[\"tuition\"])\npanel[\"cuny\"] = panel[\"institution name\"].str.contains(\"CUNY\", na=False).astype(int)\n\nprint(panel.groupby([\"fall_year\", \"state\"])[\"unitid\"].nunique().unstack())\nprint(panel[[\"ftft\", \"pell\", \"tuition\", \"unemp_lag\"]].isna().sum())"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## Helper functions\n\nThese mirror Howard's Stata workflow: `didregress` for difference-in-differences, an event study with 2016 omitted, and `estat ptrends` (a Wald test of whether New York and Pennsylvania had different linear slopes before treatment)."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "COVARS = \"log_tuition + unemp_lag\"\nEVENT_YEARS = [y for y in range(2012, 2020) if y != 2016]\n\nfor y in EVENT_YEARS:\n    panel[f\"ny_{y}\"] = panel[\"ny\"] * (panel[\"fall_year\"] == y).astype(int)\nES_TERMS = \" + \".join(f\"ny_{y}\" for y in EVENT_YEARS)\n\n\ndef fit(formula, data):\n    data = data.dropna(subset=[c for c in data.columns if c in formula])\n    return smf.ols(formula, data=data).fit(cov_type=\"cluster\", cov_kwds={\"groups\": data[\"unitid\"]})\n\n\ndef did(outcome, data=None, treat_year=2017, covariates=False):\n    \"\"\"Difference-in-differences (Howard Tables 10-11).\"\"\"\n    d = (panel if data is None else data).copy()\n    d[\"treat_post\"] = d[\"ny\"] * (d[\"fall_year\"] >= treat_year).astype(int)\n    f = f\"{outcome} ~ treat_post + C(unitid) + C(fall_year)\" + (f\" + {COVARS}\" if covariates else \"\")\n    m = fit(f, d)\n    return {\"coef\": m.params[\"treat_post\"], \"se\": m.bse[\"treat_post\"],\n            \"t\": m.tvalues[\"treat_post\"], \"p\": m.pvalues[\"treat_post\"], \"n\": int(m.nobs)}\n\n\ndef pretrend_test(outcome, data=None, treat_year=2017):\n    \"\"\"Equivalent of Stata's estat ptrends: Wald test of different NY vs. PA linear slopes before treatment (Howard Tables 8-9).\"\"\"\n    d = (panel if data is None else data)\n    d = d[d[\"fall_year\"] < treat_year].copy()\n    d[\"ny_trend\"] = d[\"ny\"] * (d[\"fall_year\"] - d[\"fall_year\"].min())\n    m = fit(f\"{outcome} ~ ny_trend + C(unitid) + C(fall_year)\", d)\n    return {\"slope_diff\": m.params[\"ny_trend\"], \"F\": m.tvalues[\"ny_trend\"] ** 2, \"p\": m.pvalues[\"ny_trend\"]}\n\n\ndef event_study(outcome, data=None, covariates=False):\n    \"\"\"Event study with 2016 as reference (Howard Table 12).\"\"\"\n    d = (panel if data is None else data)\n    f = f\"{outcome} ~ {ES_TERMS} + C(unitid) + C(fall_year)\" + (f\" + {COVARS}\" if covariates else \"\")\n    m = fit(f, d)\n    ci = m.conf_int()\n    out = pd.DataFrame({\"coef\": m.params[[f\"ny_{y}\" for y in EVENT_YEARS]],\n                        \"se\": m.bse[[f\"ny_{y}\" for y in EVENT_YEARS]]})\n    out[\"ci_low\"], out[\"ci_high\"] = ci.loc[out.index, 0], ci.loc[out.index, 1]\n    out[\"p\"] = m.pvalues[out.index]\n    out.index = EVENT_YEARS\n    pre = m.f_test(\", \".join(f\"ny_{y} = 0\" for y in [2012, 2013, 2014, 2015]))\n    post = m.f_test(\", \".join(f\"ny_{y} = 0\" for y in [2017, 2018, 2019]))\n    return out, {\"pre_F\": float(pre.fvalue), \"pre_p\": float(pre.pvalue),\n                 \"post_F\": float(post.fvalue), \"post_p\": float(post.pvalue), \"n\": int(m.nobs)}\n\n\ndef plot_event_study(es, title, ax=None):\n    ax = ax or plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 4))[1]\n    full = pd.concat([es, pd.DataFrame({\"coef\": [0], \"ci_low\": [0], \"ci_high\": [0]}, index=[2016])]).sort_index()\n    ax.errorbar(full.index, full[\"coef\"], yerr=[full[\"coef\"] - full[\"ci_low\"], full[\"ci_high\"] - full[\"coef\"]],\n                fmt=\"o\", capsize=3, color=\"black\")\n    ax.axhline(0, color=\"gray\", lw=0.8)\n    ax.axvline(2016.5, color=\"gray\", ls=\"--\", lw=0.8)\n    ax.set_title(title)\n    ax.set_xlabel(\"Fall of entry\")\n    ax.set_ylabel(\"Difference vs. 2016 (students)\")\n    return ax"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "# Part A: Replication of Howard (2026)\n\n## A1. Descriptive statistics (Howard Table 2 and Table 4 equivalent)"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "base = panel[panel[\"fall_year\"] == 2016]\ndesc = base.groupby(\"state\").agg(\n    institutions=(\"unitid\", \"nunique\"),\n    mean_ftft=(\"ftft\", \"mean\"),\n    mean_pell=(\"pell\", \"mean\"),\n    mean_tuition=(\"tuition\", \"mean\"),\n    mean_unemp=(\"unemp_lag\", \"mean\"),\n).T\ndesc"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## A2. Parallel trends: aggregate outcomes (Howard p. 61-62)"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "rows = []\nfor y in [\"ftft\", \"pell\"]:\n    for t in [2017, 2018]:\n        r = pretrend_test(y, treat_year=t)\n        rows.append({\"outcome\": y, \"treatment_year\": t, **r})\npd.DataFrame(rows)"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## A3. Difference-in-differences (Howard Tables 10-11, public institutions)\n\nHoward's public-institution estimates with treatment in 2017: FTFT 45.17 (SE 39.42) without covariates, 22.07 (49.45) with; Pell FTFT 44.18 (22.95) and 18.60 (25.70). Your sample differs (Penn State combined), so expect similar direction and size, not identical numbers."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "rows = []\nfor y in [\"ftft\", \"pell\"]:\n    for t in [2017, 2018]:\n        for cov in [False, True]:\n            rows.append({\"outcome\": y, \"treatment_year\": t, \"covariates\": cov,\n                         **did(y, treat_year=t, covariates=cov)})\ndid_table = pd.DataFrame(rows)\ndid_table"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## A4. Event study (Howard Table 12)\n\nHoward's public FTFT estimates: 2017 = 68.35, 2018 = 83.29, 2019 = 79.21; pre-trends F = 0.61, post F = 4.02."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "es_ftft, tests_ftft = event_study(\"ftft\")\nes_pell, tests_pell = event_study(\"pell\")\nprint(\"FTFT:\", {k: round(v, 3) for k, v in tests_ftft.items()})\nprint(\"Pell:\", {k: round(v, 3) for k, v in tests_pell.items()})\nes_ftft"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(12, 4), sharex=True)\nplot_event_study(es_ftft, \"First-time, full-time enrollment\", axes[0])\nplot_event_study(es_pell, \"Pell recipients (FTFT)\", axes[1])\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.savefig(\"fig2_event_study.png\", dpi=200)"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## A5. Race-specific pre-trend tests (Howard Table 8)\n\nThis is where Howard stopped. For public institutions he found Hispanic (p = .01) and White (p = .02) pre-trends diverged, so he abandoned race-specific estimates."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "rows = []\nfor g in [\"black\", \"hispanic\", \"white\"]:\n    for t in [2017, 2018]:\n        rows.append({\"group\": g, \"treatment_year\": t, **pretrend_test(g, treat_year=t)})\npd.DataFrame(rows)"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "# Part B: QuantCrit-informed extension\n\n## B1. What the categories contain (*categories are neither natural nor given*)\n\nBefore analyzing any group, document every IPEDS category, including the ones conventional analyses leave out. Watch the Unknown and Two or More Races shares: if either shifts around 2017, it can produce apparent changes in other groups."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "ALL_GROUPS = [g for g in [\"black\", \"hispanic\", \"white\", \"asian\", \"aian\", \"nhpi\", \"two_plus\", \"unknown\", \"nonresident\"]\n              if g in panel.columns]\n\ncomp = panel.groupby([\"state\", \"fall_year\"])[ALL_GROUPS + [\"ftft\"]].sum()\ncomp_share = comp[ALL_GROUPS].div(comp[\"ftft\"], axis=0) * 100\ncomp_share.round(1)"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "# check whether the categories add up to the total (gaps signal reporting problems)\ncheck = panel[ALL_GROUPS].sum(axis=1) - panel[\"ftft\"]\nprint(\"Rows where categories don't sum to total:\", (check != 0).sum())"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## B2. Three outcome measures (*numbers are not neutral*)\n\n1. **Counts**: how many students enrolled (Howard's measure)\n2. **Composition**: each group's share of an institution's first-time enrollment\n3. **Population-adjusted**: enrollment relative to the state's 12th-grade population of that group (Howard p. 66-69, CCD data advanced one year)\n\nFor measure 3 you need CCD 12th-grade counts by state, race, and year saved as `ccd_12th.csv` with columns `state, fall_year, black, hispanic, white, asian` (12th graders in fall 2015 \u2192 `fall_year` 2016). If you don't have it yet, the cell skips it."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "for g in [\"black\", \"hispanic\", \"white\", \"asian\"]:\n    panel[f\"{g}_share\"] = panel[g] / panel[\"ftft\"] * 100\n\ntry:\n    ccd = pd.read_csv(\"ccd_12th.csv\")\n    ccd = ccd.rename(columns={g: f\"{g}_12th\" for g in [\"black\", \"hispanic\", \"white\", \"asian\"]})\n    panel = panel.merge(ccd, on=[\"state\", \"fall_year\"], how=\"left\")\n    for g in [\"black\", \"hispanic\", \"white\", \"asian\"]:\n        panel[f\"{g}_per1k\"] = panel[g] / panel[f\"{g}_12th\"] * 1000   # enrollees per 1,000 12th graders\n    HAVE_CCD = True\nexcept FileNotFoundError:\n    HAVE_CCD = False\n    print(\"No CCD file yet: population-adjusted measure skipped.\")"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## Figure 1: trends by race, counts vs. composition"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "groups = [\"black\", \"hispanic\", \"white\", \"asian\"]\nlabels = {\"black\": \"Black\", \"hispanic\": \"Hispanic\", \"white\": \"White\", \"asian\": \"Asian\"}\n\ntotals = panel.groupby([\"state\", \"fall_year\"])[groups + [\"ftft\"]].sum().reset_index()\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 4, figsize=(16, 7), sharex=True)\nfor j, g in enumerate(groups):\n    for st, ls in [(\"NY\", \"-\"), (\"PA\", \"--\")]:\n        d = totals[totals[\"state\"] == st]\n        axes[0, j].plot(d[\"fall_year\"], d[g], ls, marker=\"o\", label=st, color=\"black\")\n        axes[1, j].plot(d[\"fall_year\"], d[g] / d[\"ftft\"] * 100, ls, marker=\"o\", label=st, color=\"black\")\n    axes[0, j].set_title(labels[g])\n    for i in range(2):\n        axes[i, j].axvline(2016.5, color=\"gray\", ls=\":\", lw=0.8)\naxes[0, 0].set_ylabel(\"First-time students (count)\")\naxes[1, 0].set_ylabel(\"Share of first-time students (%)\")\naxes[0, 0].legend()\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.savefig(\"fig1_trends_by_race.png\", dpi=200)"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## B3. Group-specific parallel trends across measures (*centrality of racism*)\n\nDoes Pennsylvania remain a credible counterfactual for each population, on each measure? A failed test here is a finding about the limits of the design, not a null result."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "measures = {\"count\": \"{g}\", \"share\": \"{g}_share\"}\nif HAVE_CCD:\n    measures[\"per 1k 12th graders\"] = \"{g}_per1k\"\n\nrows = []\nfor g in groups:\n    for mname, pattern in measures.items():\n        r = pretrend_test(pattern.format(g=g))\n        rows.append({\"group\": labels[g], \"measure\": mname, \"F\": r[\"F\"], \"p\": r[\"p\"],\n                     \"parallel_trends\": \"holds\" if r[\"p\"] >= 0.05 else \"FAILS\"})\npt_table = pd.DataFrame(rows)\npt_table.pivot(index=\"group\", columns=\"measure\", values=\"parallel_trends\")"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "pt_table  # full detail for your Table 6"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## B4. Event studies by group, interpreted only where pre-trends hold\n\nCoefficients for groups that fail B3 are reported but flagged, not interpreted causally."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "rows = []\nfor g in groups:\n    for mname, pattern in measures.items():\n        y = pattern.format(g=g)\n        es, tests = event_study(y)\n        match = pt_table[(pt_table[\"group\"] == labels[g]) & (pt_table[\"measure\"] == mname)]\n        ok = match[\"parallel_trends\"].iloc[0] == \"holds\"\n        rows.append({\"group\": labels[g], \"measure\": mname,\n                     \"2017\": es.loc[2017, \"coef\"], \"2018\": es.loc[2018, \"coef\"], \"2019\": es.loc[2019, \"coef\"],\n                     \"pre_F_p\": tests[\"pre_p\"], \"post_F_p\": tests[\"post_p\"],\n                     \"interpretable\": \"yes\" if ok else \"no: pre-trends fail\"})\nes_by_group = pd.DataFrame(rows)\nes_by_group"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## B5. Institutional context: CUNY vs. SUNY (*voice and insight / historical context*)\n\nNew York's public sector is not one system. CUNY enrolls a far larger share of Black and Hispanic students than SUNY. Estimating the effect separately asks whose institutions the average effect describes. Each model compares one NY system to all of Pennsylvania."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "pa = panel[panel[\"ny\"] == 0]\nsystems = {\"CUNY\": pd.concat([panel[(panel[\"ny\"] == 1) & (panel[\"cuny\"] == 1)], pa]),\n           \"SUNY / other NY public\": pd.concat([panel[(panel[\"ny\"] == 1) & (panel[\"cuny\"] == 0)], pa])}\n\n# who each system enrolls (2016)\nprint((panel[panel[\"fall_year\"] == 2016].assign(system=lambda d: np.where(d[\"ny\"] == 0, \"PA\",\n        np.where(d[\"cuny\"] == 1, \"CUNY\", \"SUNY/other\")))\n       .groupby(\"system\")[groups + [\"ftft\"]].sum()\n       .pipe(lambda d: d[groups].div(d[\"ftft\"], axis=0) * 100).round(1)))\n\nrows = []\nfor name, d in systems.items():\n    for y in [\"ftft\", \"pell\", \"black\", \"hispanic\"]:\n        pt = pretrend_test(y, data=d)\n        r = did(y, data=d)\n        rows.append({\"system\": name, \"outcome\": y, \"did_coef\": r[\"coef\"], \"se\": r[\"se\"], \"p\": r[\"p\"],\n                     \"pretrend_p\": pt[\"p\"], \"interpretable\": \"yes\" if pt[\"p\"] >= 0.05 else \"no\"})\npd.DataFrame(rows)"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## B6. The Pell result under a last-dollar design\n\nHoward reads Pell gains as benefit to lower-income students. Because Excelsior is last-dollar, many Pell recipients receive little or no award. Compare the Pell **count** effect with the Pell **share** effect: if the count rises but the share doesn't, Pell enrollment grew in step with overall enrollment, which is consistent with a broad signal (\"college is free\") more than with targeted cost relief."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "panel[\"pell_share\"] = panel[\"pell\"] / panel[\"ftft\"] * 100\npd.DataFrame([\n    {\"outcome\": \"Pell count\", **did(\"pell\")},\n    {\"outcome\": \"Pell share of FTFT (%)\", **did(\"pell_share\")},\n    {\"outcome\": \"FTFT count\", **did(\"ftft\")},\n])"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## B7. Sensitivity: how sample decisions change the estimate (*numbers are not neutral*)\n\nThe same model on alternative samples. If the estimate moves, the sample decision is doing analytic work and belongs in the decision log."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "largest = panel.groupby(\"unitid\")[\"ftft\"].mean().nlargest(5).index\nsamples = {\n    \"Main sample\": panel,\n    \"Drop combined Penn State\": panel[panel[\"unitid\"] != 214777],\n    \"Drop CUNY\": panel[panel[\"cuny\"] == 0],\n    \"Drop 5 largest institutions\": panel[~panel[\"unitid\"].isin(largest)],\n}\npd.DataFrame([{\"sample\": k, \"institutions\": d[\"unitid\"].nunique(), **did(\"ftft\", data=d)}\n              for k, d in samples.items()])"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## Save tables for the report"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "with pd.ExcelWriter(\"report_tables.xlsx\") as w:\n    desc.to_excel(w, sheet_name=\"T4_descriptives\")\n    did_table.to_excel(w, sheet_name=\"T5_did\", index=False)\n    es_ftft.to_excel(w, sheet_name=\"Fig2_es_ftft\")\n    comp_share.to_excel(w, sheet_name=\"B1_composition\")\n    pt_table.to_excel(w, sheet_name=\"T6_pretrends\", index=False)\n    es_by_group.to_excel(w, sheet_name=\"B4_es_by_group\", index=False)\nprint(\"saved report_tables.xlsx\")"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "# Part C: An intersectional transformation following L\u00f3pez et al. (2018)\n\nHoward's design treats enrollment as one undifferentiated count, which L\u00f3pez et al. (2018), drawing on Zuberi (2001), would call a decontextualized approach. This part adapts their four moves to institution-level IPEDS data:\n\n1. **Intersectional social locations.** IPEDS reports each race by men and women, so enrollment becomes race \u00d7 gender cells. Class enters through institutional context: institutions above vs. below the 2016 median Pell share (an institution-level proxy, *not* student income).\n2. **Dynamic centering.** Each social location gets its own difference-in-differences (no group is the norm), and a triple-difference model uses an explicitly chosen and justified reference group.\n3. **Missingness as a finding.** Who is in the Unknown and Nonresident categories, by state, year, and institutional context.\n4. **Radical contextualization** happens in the write-up, not the code.\n\nParts A and B are unchanged."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "import re\n\nRACES = {\"black\": \"Black or African American\", \"hispanic\": \"Hispanic\", \"white\": \"White\", \"asian\": \"Asian\"}\nGENDERS = {\"women\": \"women\", \"men\": \"men\"}\n\n# find the race x gender columns whatever their exact prefix\ndef find_col(race_label, gender):\n    pat = re.compile(rf\"^{re.escape(race_label)} {gender}$\", re.I)\n    hits = [c for c in panel.columns if pat.match(c.strip())]\n    return hits[0] if hits else None\n\ncell_cols = {}\nfor rk, rl in RACES.items():\n    for gk in GENDERS:\n        col = find_col(rl, gk)\n        if col is None:\n            print(\"MISSING column for\", rl, gk)\n        else:\n            cell_cols[f\"{rk}_{gk}\"] = col\n\nfor k, col in cell_cols.items():\n    panel[k] = pd.to_numeric(panel[col], errors=\"coerce\")\n\n# class context: institution's Pell share in 2016 (fixed, pre-treatment)\npell16 = (panel[panel[\"fall_year\"] == 2016].set_index(\"unitid\")[\"pell\"] /\n          panel[panel[\"fall_year\"] == 2016].set_index(\"unitid\")[\"ftft\"])\ncut = pell16.median()\npanel[\"high_pell_inst\"] = panel[\"unitid\"].map(pell16 >= cut).astype(int)\nprint(f\"Median 2016 Pell share = {cut:.1%}\")\nprint(panel[panel['fall_year'] == 2016].groupby(['state', 'high_pell_inst'])['unitid'].nunique().unstack())"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## C1. Who enrolls in each social location (2016)\n\nComposition of first-time enrollment by race \u00d7 gender, by state and institutional Pell context."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "SL = list(cell_cols.keys())\nlabel = lambda k: f\"{RACES[k.split('_')[0]].split(' or ')[0]} {k.split('_')[1]}\"\n\nbase16 = panel[panel[\"fall_year\"] == 2016]\ncomp_sl = base16.groupby([\"state\", \"high_pell_inst\"])[SL + [\"ftft\"]].sum()\ncomp_sl = comp_sl[SL].div(comp_sl[\"ftft\"], axis=0).mul(100).round(1)\ncomp_sl.columns = [label(k) for k in comp_sl.columns]\ncomp_sl = comp_sl.rename(index={0: \"Lower-Pell\", 1: \"Higher-Pell\"}, level=\"high_pell_inst\")\ncomp_sl"
